{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-89347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/a2f0f570ecc68de1a5b34fb3bd1b341fc83a728b026b035dde848dbf986e441b.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/e7ef7fd90c828767359daf69f68f406d61e3f82ad86b1f6b3a9480c99dedadf8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ we get the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5114d6e8987a4b7de8923d778dd9011ea75fe6c651ba6d054232f0ff6f8ddcaf"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]"] ["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-89347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/999d1d14a31f9619306efe6b1c2ebc6e900a52bf4e2d2edd7230e7dd7c9b25b9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(1,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\frac{b}{1} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5114d6e8987a4b7de8923d778dd9011ea75fe6c651ba6d054232f0ff6f8ddcaf"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/b570ce72a87d7583a230ea1c863656f982c7fefeb546295d41429876cc823837.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(2,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=2.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5114d6e8987a4b7de8923d778dd9011ea75fe6c651ba6d054232f0ff6f8ddcaf"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] ["math" "x=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5114d6e8987a4b7de8923d778dd9011ea75fe6c651ba6d054232f0ff6f8ddcaf"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-106524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-325941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-325941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{10} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-325941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5114d6e8987a4b7de8923d778dd9011ea75fe6c651ba6d054232f0ff6f8ddcaf"} {"question" "q-325941" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"} {"question" "q-325941" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{100}-\\frac{y^2}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/state.json" "file_sha256" "411018d17b5133b3000d9dda7d28fd1b979521367e556284430679b0cb87903f"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1867 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
